ch \af5 \loch \f5 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025  Die Meistersinger}{\rtlch \fcs1 \af0 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 ?\uc1 \u160 ?And how much of the former work may perhaps still echo in the latter?}{\cs15 \fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \af5 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \super1 \chftn {\footnote }}{\rtlch \fcs1 \af0 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025  But there is no doubt that this \uc1 \u8220 ?Luther\uc1 \u8217 ?s Wedding\uc1 \u8221 ? would also have involved the praise of chastity. Of course, it would have contained a praise of sensuality, as well\uc1 \u8212 ?and that, it strikes me, would have been very much in order, very\uc1 \u8220 ?Wagnerian,\uc1 \u8221 ? too. For between chastity and sensuality there is no necessary opposition. Every good marriage, every genuine affair of the heart transcends this opposition. In my view, Wagner would have\uc1 \u160 ?done well if he had enabled his Germans to take this}{\rtlch \fcs1 \af0 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \i1 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025  pleasant\uc1 \u160 ?}{\rtlch \fcs1 \af0 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 fact to heart once more, with the help of a lovely and brave comedy about Luther, for among the Germans there are and always have been a lot of people who slander sensuality, and Luther\uc1 \u8217 ?s merit is probably nowhere greater than precisely here: in having had the courage of his own }{\rtlch \fcs1 \af0 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \i1 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 sensuality }{\rtlch \fcs1 \af0 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 (\uc1 \u8212 ?at that time people called it, delicately enough, \uc1 \u8220 ?evangelical freedom\uc1 \u8221 ?). But even if it were the case that there really is that antithesis between chastity and sensuousness, fortunately there is no need for it to be a tragic antithesis. At least this should be the case for all successful and cheerful mortals, who are far from considering their unstable equilibrium between \uc1 \u8220 ?animal and angel\uc1 \u8221 ? an immediate argument against existence\uc1 \u8212 ?the finest and brightest, like Goethe, like Hafiz, even saw in this one}{\rtlch \fcs1 \af0 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \i1 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025  more\uc1 \u160 ?}{\rtlch \fcs1 \af0 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 attraction of life.}{\cs15 \fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \af5 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \super1 \chftn {\footnote \pard \plain \s20 \li0 \ri0 \lisb0 \sa60 \lisa0 \nosnaplinegrid \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \rtlch \fcs1 \af0 \afs18 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs18 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af4 \loch \af4 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 {\rtlch \fcs1 \af5 \afs20 \ltrch \fcs0 \cs15 \fs20 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \af5 \super1 \chftn }{\rtlch \fcs1 \af5 \afs20 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs20 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 Hafiz: Muhammad Schamaddhin (1330\uc1 \u8211 ?89), Persian poet who inspired Goethe\uc1 \u8217 ?s cycle of poems }{\rtlch \fcs1 \af5 \afs20 \ltrch \fcs0 \i1 \fs20 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 West-Eastern Divan }{\rtlch \fcs1 \af5 \afs20 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs20 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 (1819, 1827), which functioned throughout the nineteenth century as an influential example}{\rtlch \fcs1 \af5 \afs20 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs20 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025  }{\rtlch \fcs1 \af5 \afs20 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs20 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 of how the cultures of the Occident and the Orient could be synthesized.}}}{\rtlch \fcs1 \af0 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025  It\uc1 \u8217 ?s precisely such \uc1 \u8220 ?contradictions\uc1 \u8221 ? that make existence enticing.... On the other hand, it\uc1 \u8217 ?s easy enough to understand that once pigs who have had bad luck are persuaded to worship chastity\uc1 \u8212 ?and there are such swine!\uc1 \u8212 ?they see in chastity only their opposite, the opposite to unlucky pigs, and will worship that\uc1 \u8212 ?and with such zealous tragic grunting! We can imagine it\uc1 \u8212 ?that embarrassing and unnecessary antithesis, which Richard Wagner at the end of his life unquestioningly still wanted to set to music and produce on stage.\uc1 \u160 ?}{\rtlch \fcs1 \af0 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \i1 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 But what for}{\rtlch \fcs1 \af0 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 ? That\uc1 \u8217 ?s a fair question. For why should he be concerned about pigs? Why should we?\uc1 \u8212 ?}{\rtlch \fcs1 \af0 \afs24 \ltrch 