ristians. To sense the contrast, we should remember something about Luther, the \uc1 \u8220 ?most eloquent\uc1 \u8221 ? and most presumptuous peasant Germany ever had, and the tone Luther adopted as the one he most preferred in his conversations with God. Luther\uc1 \u8217 ?s resistance to the interceding saints of the church (especially to \uc1 \u8220 ?the devil\uc1 \u8217 ?s sow, the Pope\uc1 \u8221 ?) was undoubtedly, in the last analysis, the resistance of a lout irritated by the}{\rtlch \fcs1 \af0 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \i1 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025  good etiquette\uc1 \u160 ?}{\rtlch \fcs1 \af0 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 of the church, that etiquette of reverence of the priestly taste, which lets only the more consecrated and the more discreet into the holy of holies and shuts the door against the louts, who in this particular place are never to speak. But Luther, the peasant, simply wanted something different\uc1 \u8212 ?this situation was not }{\rtlch \fcs1 \af0 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \i1 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 German }{\rtlch \fcs1 \af0 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 enough for him. Above all, he wanted to speak directly, to speak for himself, to speak \uc1 \u8220 ?openly\uc1 \u8221 ? with his God. Well, he did it.\uc1 \u8212 ?You can, I think, conjecture easily enough that there has never been a place anywhere in which the ascetic ideal has been a school of good taste, even less of good manners\uc1 \u8212 ?in the best cases, it was a school for priestly manners. That comes about because it carries something in its own body which is the deadly enemy 